WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.390
Small leaks may become serious in
processes that run for hours, days, weeks,

2
00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:05.017
or months.

3
00:00:05.097 --> 00:00:09.230
A missed release in a rarely used branch
may not show up during a short manual test.

4
00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:13.625
Under repeated workload, the same branch
can steadily increase memory use until the

5
00:00:13.705 --> 00:00:15.347
process becomes less reliable.

6
00:00:15.427 --> 00:00:18.405
Services, daemons, embedded systems, batch
processors,

7
00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:21.949
and long-lived tools may accumulate leaks
under normal operation.

8
00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:25.676
A leak that occurs once per request, once
per file, once per connection,

9
00:00:25.756 --> 00:00:30.072
or once per periodic refresh can be enough
to create operational problems over time.

10
00:00:30.152 --> 00:00:34.285
Libraries have a special responsibility
because they run inside someone else's

11
00:00:34.365 --> 00:00:34.851
process.

12
00:00:34.931 --> 00:00:39.003
A library should avoid shifting unclear
cleanup responsibilities to callers.

13
00:00:39.083 --> 00:00:43.459
If a library allocates and returns memory,
the release routine should be documented.

14
00:00:43.539 --> 00:00:45.059
If a caller provides storage,

15
00:00:45.139 --> 00:00:49.394
the library should not silently keep
ownership unless that is part of the contract.

16
00:00:49.474 --> 00:00:51.115
Shutdown cleanup may be useful,

17
00:00:51.195 --> 00:00:54.294
but it does not excuse leaks during
steady-state operation.

18
00:00:54.374 --> 00:00:57.900
Releasing everything at process exit can
make diagnostics quieter,

19
00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:01.930
but a service that grows continuously
during runtime still has a reliability

20
00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:02.497
problem.

21
00:01:02.577 --> 00:01:05.859
The steady-state path matters most for
long-running software.

22
00:01:05.939 --> 00:01:08.856
Operational symptoms may include growing
memory usage,

23
00:01:08.936 --> 00:01:12.705
degraded performance, allocator pressure,
restarts, failed requests,

24
00:01:12.785 --> 00:01:14.061
or resource exhaustion.

25
00:01:14.141 --> 00:01:18.396
These symptoms can be hard to connect to
one code path unless allocation and cleanup

26
00:01:18.476 --> 00:01:20.360
behavior are observable and tested.

27
00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.904
Leak prevention supports reliability, not
just code cleanliness.

28
00:01:23.984 --> 00:01:26.841
A codebase with clear ownership,
predictable cleanup,

29
00:01:26.921 --> 00:01:30.021
and tested error paths is easier to
operate, easier to debug,

30
00:01:30.101 --> 00:01:33.140
and safer to extend when new features add
more allocations.
